//@version=6
indicator("UCTPROBE_P1_TOP_UNMEASURED", overlay = false)

// ⭐⭐ PHASE 1, WAVE 1 — THE ENGINE'S MOST LOAD-BEARING UNMEASURED CLAIMS
//
// `vendorParityCoverage.measure.test.js` names 29 declared functions that real
// corpus scripts call and no committed probe has ever measured. This probe
// takes the top of that list by demand:
//
//     abs 95 scripts · nz 94 · atr 73 · change 72 · ema 65 · stdev 27 · rma 21
//
// ⛔⛔ EVERY READING HERE IS CHOSEN TO DISCRIMINATE, NOT TO EXERCISE.
// `r11-nine-safe.pine` states the rule this file follows: *"The positives tell
// you nothing: ceil(2.5)=3 and floor(2.5)=2 under every rule anyone has ever
// shipped."* A reading both implementations agree on by construction proves
// nothing and costs a plot slot. So each block below names the SPECIFIC
// disagreement it can see, and several carry a control that must come back
// EQUAL — because a probe where everything differs is as uninformative as one
// where nothing does.
//
// ⚠️ RUN IT ON A LIQUID DAILY SYMBOL WITH DEEP HISTORY (SPY 1D). The seeding
// questions below are answered in the FIRST ~20 BARS of the series and nowhere
// else; a capture that only records the tail cannot see them.

// ── 1. `nz` — WHAT DOES IT SUBSTITUTE, AND FOR WHAT? (94 scripts) ───────────
// Two separate questions this engine currently answers without evidence:
//   (a) the one-argument form's substitute — 0, or something else?
//   (b) does the two-argument form take the REPLACEMENT or the VALUE when the
//       value is present? An implementation that has them backwards passes every
//       test written with `nz(na, x)` and fails on every real call site.
plot(nz(na), "N01_nz_of_na_expect_0")                                        // N01
plot(nz(na, 42.0), "N02_nz_na_with_replacement_expect_42")                   // N02
plot(nz(7.0, 42.0), "N03_nz_PRESENT_with_replacement_expect_7_NOT_42")       // N03

// ── 2. `math.abs` — THE na QUESTION, NOT THE SIGN QUESTION (95 scripts) ─────
// ⛔ `abs(-2.5) = 2.5` is agreed by every implementation ever written and is
// here ONLY as the control that proves the probe is reading this function at
// all. The real question is `abs(na)`: na-propagating, or 0?
plot(math.abs(-2.5), "N04_abs_neg2p5_CONTROL_expect_2p5")                    // N04
plot(math.abs(na), "N05_abs_of_na_THE_QUESTION_na_or_0")                     // N05

// ── 3. `ta.change` — THE VALUE ON BAR 0 (72 scripts) ────────────────────────
// `ta.change(x)` is `x - x[1]`, and on the FIRST bar there is no `x[1]`.
// na, or 0? Every script that sums or counts changes diverges on bar 0 if this
// is wrong, and the divergence is invisible on every later bar.
plot(ta.change(close), "N06_change_close_bar0_is_the_reading")               // N06
plot(bar_index, "N07_bar_index_SO_BAR_0_IS_IDENTIFIABLE")                    // N07

// ── 4. `ta.ema` — THE SEED. THIS IS THE BIG ONE (65 scripts) ────────────────
// ⭐⭐ EMA implementations differ in exactly one place and it is not the
// recurrence, it is what the recurrence STARTS from:
//     (a) seed = the first source value            -> ema[n-1] != sma[n-1]
//     (b) seed = SMA of the first n values         -> ema[n-1] == sma[n-1]
// Plotting both and comparing them AT BAR n-1 answers it in one reading. On
// every later bar they diverge under both rules, so the tail cannot answer it.
// ⛔ A capture that starts after bar 5 here is WORTHLESS for this question.
plot(ta.ema(close, 5), "N08_ema5_SEED_QUESTION_compare_to_N09_at_bar4")      // N08
plot(ta.sma(close, 5), "N09_sma5_THE_DISCRIMINATOR_FOR_N08")                 // N09

// ── 5. `ta.rma` and `ta.atr` — WILDER SMOOTHING, AND ARE THEY THE SAME? ─────
// (atr 73 scripts, rma 21)
// Two claims this engine makes and has never checked:
//   (a) rma's own seed — same question as ema, different constant;
//   (b) that `ta.atr(n)` IS `ta.rma(ta.tr(true), n)`. If those two plots ever
//       differ by more than float noise, one of them is wrong and every ATR in
//       the corpus is suspect.
// ⭐ N11 vs N12 is an IDENTITY control: it must come back EQUAL. A probe where
// every reading differs is as uninformative as one where none does.
plot(ta.atr(5), "N10_atr5")                                                  // N10
plot(ta.rma(ta.tr(true), 5), "N11_rma_of_tr5_MUST_EQUAL_N10")                // N11
plot(ta.sma(ta.tr(true), 5), "N12_sma_of_tr5_SEED_DISCRIMINATOR")            // N12

// ── 6. `ta.stdev` — POPULATION OR SAMPLE? (27 scripts) ──────────────────────
// ⭐⭐ THE CLASSIC DIVERGENCE, and it is a whole-series offset rather than a
// seeding artefact: dividing by `n` (population) or `n-1` (sample) differs on
// EVERY bar, by a factor of sqrt(n/(n-1)) = 1.118 at n=5. Large enough to see,
// small enough that nobody notices it by eye in a chart — which is precisely
// how a wrong one ships.
plot(ta.stdev(close, 5), "N13_stdev5_POPULATION_OR_SAMPLE")                  // N13
// ⚠️ `ta.variance` IS NOT DECLARED BY THIS ENGINE, and this probe found that by
// being run through our own translator BEFORE anyone was asked to spend a
// TradingView session on it (`pine:function @ ta.variance`, line 80). It stays
// OUT so that every reading here is one BOTH sides can produce — a capture of a
// name we cannot translate yields a vendor number with nothing to compare it
// against, which is a reading that cannot settle anything. Recorded as its own
// vocabulary gap instead, for the Phase 2 queue.

// ── 7. `math.sum` — BEFORE THE WINDOW FILLS (40 scripts) ────────────────────
// A running sum over n bars has no defined answer until n bars exist. na, or a
// partial sum of what there is? Scripts that divide by the sum diverge hard on
// the early bars if this is wrong.
plot(math.sum(close, 5), "N15_sum5_PRE_WINDOW_na_or_partial")                // N15

// ⚠️ NOT IN THIS PROBE, DELIBERATELY, AND THE REASON IS ITEM 7'S SPLIT RULE:
// `rsi`, `macd`, `stoch`, `cci`, `hma`, `wma`, `percentrank`, `mfi`, `atan`,
// `avwap`. Each is a COMPOSITE whose answer depends on the seeding questions
// above — measuring them before rma/ema/stdev are settled would produce
// readings nobody can attribute. They are wave 2, after this probe's answers
// land.
